    {
      "id": "Gs5X2nfN_kNBOHyZ9-WdO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3655.2078455353553,
      "y": 33922.14204268059,
      "width": 356.982151034606,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bAU",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 31778805,
      "version": 174,
      "versionNonce": 2054735628,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786596117100,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Slope is M\n\ny = mx + c\n\nlet's say m = 2\n\ny = 2x + c\n\ny2 - y1 = m(x2 - x1)\n\n5 - y = m (3 - x)\n\ny = 5 - 6 + 2x\ny = 2x - 1",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Slope is M\n\ny = mx + c\n\nlet's say m = 2\n\ny = 2x + c\n\ny2 - y1 = m(x2 - x1)\n\n5 - y = m (3 - x)\n\ny = 5 - 6 + 2x\ny = 2x - 1",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "KFX2GiIoUBl9BP7TLJsPN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2494.1510608511053,
      "y": 33916.47746781987,
      "width": 1588.838623046875,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bAV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1617167756,
      "version": 2438,
      "versionNonce": 115143436,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786598616304,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Linear Algebra\n\nVector:\n    - In computer science perspective, an ordered list of numbers\n    - In physics, an arrow which is solely defined by it's length and direction. It doesn't matter where it starts\n    - In LA, an arrow that is anchored at origin. The coordiantes tell you how far to step along each direction to reach the arrow's tip.\n\nScalar: Any real number, C. when multiplied with vector, it can stretch, shrink or reverse direction of the vector.\n\nVector Addition: Placing vectors tip-to-tail to get the combined effect of the addition (v + w).\n\nLinear Dependence: A set of vectors is called Linearly dependent, iff atleast one vector can be represented as sum of scaled versions of other vectors in the set.\n    i.e {v1, v2, v3} is linearly dependent, iff c1v1 = c2v2 + c3v3, where c1 != 0.\n\n-> If a set of vectors is not linearly dependent, then it is linearly independent.\n\n-> c1v1 + c2v2 + c3v3 + ... + cnvn = 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly dependent iff atleast one of ci != 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly Independent iff the trivial solution c1 = c2 = ... = cn is the only trivial solution that satisifies the equation.\n\nSpan: Span of a set of vectors is the set of all possible vectors that can be represented as linear combinations.\n\n-> Span of a single vector in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n-> Span of two non-collinear vectors in 2D Space: The entire 2D space (R2)\n-> Span of two collinear vectors in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n\nBasis: Basis of a vector space is the set of vectors that are linearly independent and can span entire space.\n\nDimension: is the number of vectors in the basis of a space. R2 has 2 dimension, coz, the basis has 2 vectors.\n\nUnit Vectors: The set of n vectors in Rn space, where in each vector only only co-ordinate has a value 1 and rest has a value 0. They also form a basis.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Linear Algebra\n\nVector:\n    - In computer science perspective, an ordered list of numbers\n    - In physics, an arrow which is solely defined by it's length and direction. It doesn't matter where it starts\n    - In LA, an arrow that is anchored at origin. The coordiantes tell you how far to step along each direction to reach the arrow's tip.\n\nScalar: Any real number, C. when multiplied with vector, it can stretch, shrink or reverse direction of the vector.\n\nVector Addition: Placing vectors tip-to-tail to get the combined effect of the addition (v + w).\n\nLinear Dependence: A set of vectors is called Linearly dependent, iff atleast one vector can be represented as sum of scaled versions of other vectors in the set.\n    i.e {v1, v2, v3} is linearly dependent, iff c1v1 = c2v2 + c3v3, where c1 != 0.\n\n-> If a set of vectors is not linearly dependent, then it is linearly independent.\n\n-> c1v1 + c2v2 + c3v3 + ... + cnvn = 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly dependent iff atleast one of ci != 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly Independent iff the trivial solution c1 = c2 = ... = cn is the only trivial solution that satisifies the equation.\n\nSpan: Span of a set of vectors is the set of all possible vectors that can be represented as linear combinations.\n\n-> Span of a single vector in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n-> Span of two non-collinear vectors in 2D Space: The entire 2D space (R2)\n-> Span of two collinear vectors in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n\nBasis: Basis of a vector space is the set of vectors that are linearly independent and can span entire space.\n\nDimension: is the number of vectors in the basis of a space. R2 has 2 dimension, coz, the basis has 2 vectors.\n\nUnit Vectors: The set of n vectors in Rn space, where in each vector only only co-ordinate has a value 1 and rest has a value 0. They also form a basis.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
